# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LMDB index environments (2026-07-14)
#
# LMDB is a DERIVED index backend. It is materialized from the CDX container
# via SETLMDB (see src/cli/cmd_setlmdb.cpp: DbArea -> IndexManager ->
# CdxBackend -> LMDB env, stored as <stem>.cdx.d/).
#
# Measured 53 GB in dottalkpp/data/lmdb on 2026-07-14. It must never reach
# C:\x64base or GitHub. Publish the CDX; regenerate the LMDB locally with
# dottalkpp/data/scripts/mcc/mcc_build_x64_lmdb.dts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/dottalkpp/data/lmdb/
**/*.cdx.d/
# .mdb (LMDB env files) anywhere, not just under data/lmdb/. All OTHER valid
# dottalkpp file types (.dbf/.cdx/.dtx/.fpt/.dbt/.meta/...) are let THROUGH -- they
# may be tracked as fixtures/source tables. Only .mdb is excluded, because it is
# regenerable from the CDX container (SETLMDB) and can be huge. Matches the
# prepush-gate hard block on .mdb (member.derald ruling 2026-08-05).
**/*.mdb

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Engine state under the SYS slot (2026-09-12)
#
# GROUPS.dbf and GROUPMEM.dbf are the multi-area commit group log and its
# members table (AIF-160; src/cli/group_log.cpp:183 and :281). They are
# PER-MACHINE ENGINE STATE and not fixtures: every decision row is keyed by the
# committing process's own owner token (host:pid:nonce, reused from
# xbase::locks::current_owner), and every member row names an absolute table
# path on THIS disk. Carried into another tree those rows describe processes
# that never ran and files that do not exist.
#
# NOTHING IS LOST BY EXCLUDING THEM. group_log.cpp's ensure_table() creates
# either file on first write, and the read paths deliberately never do -- "a
# lookup must never CREATE the authority it is asking" (:239). A fresh clone
# mints both the first time someone commits a group.
#
# THIS IS THE EXCEPTION THE .mdb NOTE ABOVE DID NOT ANTICIPATE. That note lets
# every other dottalkpp file type through on the grounds that a .dbf may be a
# tracked fixture -- true everywhere EXCEPT the one slot that exists to hold
# state nothing can rebuild and nothing sweeps. Deleting a row here silently
# loses a committed transaction; every other loss in this tree announces itself.
#
# Denied BY EXTENSION rather than by directory, so a doctrine note or README
# under data/sys stays trackable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/dottalkpp/data/sys/*.dbf
/dottalkpp/data/sys/*.dtx
/dottalkpp/data/sys/*.cdx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Engine-written posture artifacts under data/workspaces (2026-09-12)
#
# WORKSPACE SAVE <name> writes <name>.dtschema into the workspaces root. Six
# such OUTPUTS were tracked in 35612d078 on the strength of cmd_regression.cpp
# NAMING them -- and a grep for a name cannot tell a read from a write. That is
# the same defect the closure check was built to kill, run backwards. Each file
# below is produced by the spec that mentions it:
#
#   pk_posture.dtschema       workspace_load_posture_keys.dts:192  SAVE .. V3
#   topo_chain.dtschema       mcc_topology_workspaces.dts:107      SAVE
#   topo_deep.dtschema        mcc_topology_workspaces.dts:170      SAVE
#   topo_fan.dtschema         mcc_topology_workspaces.dts:227      SAVE
#   topo_diamond.dtschema     mcc_topology_workspaces.dts:277      SAVE
#   topo_flat.dtschema        mcc_topology_workspaces.dts:324      SAVE
#   keyregr_sandbox.dtschema  key_metadata_regression.dts:46       SAVE
#
# keyregr_sandbox was never tracked. It is named here so the CLASS is closed
# rather than the six instances that happened to get committed -- in a repo
# with status.showUntrackedFiles=no, an untracked artifact is one `git add -A`
# away from becoming a tracked one, and nobody would see it happen.
#
# TRACKING ONE OF THESE IS NOT MERELY NOISE. pk_posture is the only DTSHEMA 3
# posture in the tree, and a v3 posture carries its own DBFROOT/IDXROOT/LMDBROOT
# lines -- so the committed copy shipped three D:\code\ccode paths into a
# repository other people clone (LOCAL-PATH GUARD, OI-017 class).
#
# IT COULD NOT BE REPAIRED IN PLACE. .dtschema is read by cmd_workspace.cpp's
# own parser (workspace_load_from_stream, :2613), which performs NO macro
# substitution: DBFROOT is taken as a literal path and resolve_member_dbf()
# either uses it absolute or joins it to the SETPATH slot. The &_DBF /
# &_INDEXES / &_LMDB treatment that sterilised the seven .dts files has no
# meaning in this format. The roots are machine-absolute BECAUSE THE ENGINE
# WROTE THEM ON THIS MACHINE. The file is the wrong thing to fix; tracking it
# was the defect.
#
# All seven are rewritten by their spec on the next run, and the fixtures
# pk_posture points at (DBF/SANDBOX/PKA, DBF/SANDBOX/PKB) are ERASED at the end
# of that run (workspace_load_posture_keys.dts:260-265), so a committed copy is
# stale the moment it lands.
#
# HAND-AUTHORED POSTURES ARE NOT AFFECTED and stay tracked. cascade_all,
# mcc_x64, metadata, my_custom_workspace and the rest are INPUTS -- the owner's
# preset minidb schemas, "a versatile and important schema" (ruling
# 2026-09-12) -- and cascade_env_regression.dts:30 says so in as many words:
# "Requires workspaces/cascade_all.dtschema".
#
# Denied BY NAME rather than by pattern. A glob over *.dtschema here would deny
# the presets too, and *_sandbox / topo_* would be a guess about future names.
# A spec that starts writing a new posture must add its name here; that is a
# deliberate cost, paid once, by the person who knows it is an output.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/pk_posture.dtschema
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/topo_chain.dtschema
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/topo_deep.dtschema
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/topo_diamond.dtschema
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/topo_fan.dtschema
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/topo_flat.dtschema
/dottalkpp/data/workspaces/keyregr_sandbox.dtschema

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIELDMGR engine scratch tables, tree-wide (AIF-133; ignored here 2026-09-12)
#
# A FIELDMGR restructure writes two extra tables beside the real one and both
# keep the .dbf extension:
#
#     STUDENTS.dbf  ->  STUDENTS.__fldtmp.dbf   the rewrite in progress
#                       STUDENTS.__fldbak.dbf   the pre-change backup, KEPT
#
# THE ENGINE ALREADY KNOWS THESE ARE NOT TABLES AND GIT DID NOT. AIF-133 taught
# every directory scan to skip them -- dottalk::is_engine_scratch_table() in
# include/dottalk/scratch_sidecar.hpp, matching the marker as a trailing
# segment of the STEM (the extension really is .dbf) and case-insensitively.
# Nothing taught the deny-list, so a class the engine deliberately hides stayed
# one `git add -A` from being published, and with status.showUntrackedFiles=no
# in this repo it would have happened in silence.
#
# WHAT AIF-133 MEASURED IS WHY THESE MUST NOT SHIP. `.__fldbak` sorts BEFORE
# `.dbf`, so before the fix the backup did not merely tag along -- it took
# AREA 0 and shifted every area number after it:
#
#     Area 0: opened 'REALTAB.__fldbak.dbf'    <- the BACKUP is the ACTIVE area
#     Area 1: opened 'REALTAB.__FLDTMP.dbf'
#     Area 2: opened 'REALTAB.dbf'
#
# A posture saved from that state records a backup as the active table, and a
# MINIDB save carries its bytes into the container (22,425 B, 17% of one
# observed payload) where WRITEBACK puts them back on disk.
#
# A BACKUP THAT CANNOT RESTORE IS NOT WORTH PUBLISHING EITHER, measured on
# dottalkpp/data/workspaces/WORKSPACES.__fldbak.dbf 2026-09-12: 21 fields and
# 273 rows against the live table's 22 and 282, with all 20 pre-existing
# columns byte-identical and PARENT_ID normalized blank -> 0 by the same append
# that added TREE_DEPTH. It has NO .__fldbak.dtx of its own, so its SNAPSHOT
# memo pointers -- byte-identical to the live table's -- read through to the
# LIVE sidecar, which now holds 282 rows against this header's 273. It is a
# structure snapshot, not a recovery copy, and 196 KB of binary that cannot
# restore is pure weight in a repository other people clone.
#
# Sidecars are matched too (`.*` rather than `.dbf`): a scratch table's own
# .dtx/.cdx would be the same class, and denying the table while publishing its
# index is the half-fix that reads as a corrupt pair.
#
# THE BRACKET CLASSES ARE NOT DECORATION -- THEY ARE THE ONE HOLE AIF-133
# NAMED IN ADVANCE, AND A PLAIN LOWER-CASE PATTERN WALKS STRAIGHT INTO IT.
# scratch_sidecar.hpp says so in as many words: "a guard that missed
# `.__FLDBAK.DBF` would have a hole in exactly the case nobody tests. The
# verification run used one upper-case form for that reason." The C++ predicate
# lowercases the stem before comparing; GITIGNORE HAS NO SUCH STEP and no (?i)
# flag, so `**/*.__fldbak.*` matches the lower-case spelling ONLY. Measured
# 2026-09-12 against real git in a scratch repo: `REALTAB.__FLDBAK.DBF` came
# back TRACKED under the plain pattern and IGNORED under the class form. The
# marker's own header predicted this file's bug before it was written.
#
# core.ignorecase=true on a Windows checkout would paper over it locally, which
# is the worst possible outcome: green here, a published backup on the first
# clone taken on Linux or macOS. The classes cover the marker's letters; `__`
# is literal and the extension is covered by the trailing `.*`.
#
# ADD A NEW SCRATCH FORM IN scratch_sidecar.hpp FIRST -- that header owns the
# naming convention and is the one place the writer and every reader share a
# spelling -- then mirror it here, in the class form. Two markers in two files
# is the cost of git not being able to call a C++ predicate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
**/*.__[fF][lL][dD][bB][aA][kK].*
**/*.__[fF][lL][dD][tT][mM][pP].*
